Vitreous Bop
Ingredients: BEBOP + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Bells + 1990s ERA
Creative Catalyst: It was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule.

It sounds like a frantic 1940s jazz band trapped inside a melting cathode-ray tube, where dizzying, mathematically impossible horn runs are played backward over a hazy, slowed-down 1990s boom-bap drum loop while cathedral bells melt into undulating acid-house synthesizer blobs. 

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 78 BPM, swinging with a heavy, dragging physical weight that feels perpetually on the verge of collapsing into slow motion.
- Harmonic Palette: Altered dominant scales, chromatic clusters, and microtonal bell-overtones that evoke a vintage jazz club sinking into a swamp of liquid crystal.
- Production Signatures: 1) Bit-crushed tape warp, 2) Resonant church-bell sidechaining, 3) Stereo-panned horn isolation in 12-bit samplers.
- Dynamics: Songs open with a sterile, glassy chime before exploding into violent, unhinged horn improvisations that suddenly swallow themselves in a wash of reverse tape delay.

Scene & Origin Lore
In a damp basement in late-1998 Prague, a collective of disillusioned jazz archivists and rogue hardware modifiers plugged a bizarre, oxide-shedding Maxell cassette into a heavily circuit-bent Akai MPC2000. The tape—retrieved from a time capsule buried during an unexplained meteorological anomaly—dictated a musical language that defied linear time, blending the aggressive intellectualism of bebop with the hallucinatory visual smear of 90s trip-hop. The subculture adopted a strict sartorial code: heavy trench coats lined with broken watch parts, tinted amber spectacles that made everything look like an old photograph left in the sun, and the ritualistic ringing of small bronze handbells before speaking in public.

Typical Instruments: Altered trumpet, mutated upright bass samples, vintage 1990s Rompler church bells, analog tape-delay units, and circuit-bent drum machines.

